# ---------------------------------
#   pinned launchers
# ---------------------------------
#
# One DESKTOP-ENTRY ID per line — the file name under
# /usr/share/applications or ~/.local/share/applications, without the
# `.desktop`. Two surfaces read this file and they read the same one: the
# taskbar's quick-launch row, left of the window list, and the Start menu's
# pinned column above the rule. Two lists of favourites would be two things to
# keep in agreement, and one of them always loses.
#
# An id with no entry is skipped in silence: an alien app that is not in this
# image's appbox bake must not leave a launcher that opens nothing.
#
# SHIP A LIST, not an empty file. The quick-launch row and the pinned column
# both read this, and both of them look broken on a freshly booted machine
# when it is empty. Delete every line for a bare desktop.
#
# The first three are terminal entries, which is what makes them the right
# default: they are the same rows on the console desktop as under the
# compositor, where `foot` is a Wayland client and the boxed three need a bake.

# A line is a desktop-entry id, optionally followed by `code=XX` — the
# two-letter shorthand DESQview's Open Window menu had. Typing both letters in
# the Start menu opens that row: no arrows, no Enter, and no waiting to see
# whether the search narrowed to one. The code is drawn on the row, because a
# code nobody can see is one nobody learns.
#
# `foot` and `kdos-term` both mean THE TERMINAL and resolve to whichever this
# desktop runs, so one file serves the console and the compositor.
mc code=FM
btop code=MO
lazygit code=GI
foot code=TE
firefox-esr code=WW
org.xfce.mousepad code=ED
gimp code=IM
